  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAdded a few counties to today's Red Flag Warning, covering all areas \nalong and south of Hwy 14. After the northwest winds increase behind \nthe sfc low, dewpoints behind the dry line to the south of the low \nwill not rise. That puts southern Hand county in min RH of 20 to 25 \npercent. While not hitting strict RH criteria in the added counties, \nthe 30 to 40 mph wind gusts are sufficiently high enough for high to \nvery high grassland fire danger and merited the headline. \n\nHi-res models continue to be less than impressed with storm chances \nin northeast SD this evening keeping everything in west central MN \nstarting at 0z. Still, with dewpoints in the 60s ahead of the low \nand steep lapse rates, if anything does develop around the cold \nfront, it will be severe. Looking at a relatively short window of \ntime, though, with the fropa. Strong winds and large hail look to be \nthe main threats, especially back in SD. \n\nMuch cooler, fall-like temperatures push in behind the low with \nhighs in the 60s to lower 70s. As another low moves into the Plains \nSunday and Monday, rain and clouds could suppress temperatures even \nmore, perhaps holding them in the 50s. There is a marginal risk on \nSunday for areas south of Hwy 12. That's generally associated more \nwith a potential 30 to 40 kt llj and passing shortwaves Sunday night \nas instability/forcing will be limited otherwise. Additional chances \nfor rain arrive on Wednesday before high pressure and upper ridging \nbuild in for Thursday.",
